# Section index: 0=Overview, 1=Architecture/stack, 2=Data model, 3=Auth/security,
# 4=Catalog/inventory, 5=Devices/credentials, 6=Sync, 7=Payments, 8=Reports,
# 9=Admin app, 10=POS app, 11=Testing/deployment/roadmap
QA1 = [
(0, "What problem does this project solve?",
 "It gives a Kenyan retail business one system for tills, stock and M-Pesa payments instead of three disconnected tools. A central backend holds the ledger and catalog, the POS app sells even when the shop loses network, and the admin app gives the owner a live view of every branch. The core promise is a single source of truth for stock and money across branches, with reconciliation when things go wrong.",
 "project-description-revised.md §1; backend/src/routes/*.ts"),
(0, "Who are the system's users and what can each do?",
 "There are two server roles: OWNER (sees and manages every branch, creates users) and MANAGER (scoped to assigned branches). Cashiers are not server accounts — they operate the POS and are recorded per sale via cashier_id and cashier_name. This keeps admin auth tight while the till stays simple to operate.",
 "backend/src/lib/jwt.ts (Role); backend/src/routes/auth.ts; backend/src/routes/sync.ts (sale.cashier_*); API_CONTRACT.md §1"),
(0, "What are the three main components?",
 "1) The backend API (backend/) — Fastify + PostgreSQL, deployed on Render, holding auth, catalog, devices, sync, payments, reconciliation and reports. 2) orangeadmin — the Android admin app for the owner and managers. 3) orangemain — the Kotlin Multiplatform POS app used by cashiers, offline-first with a local database.",
 "backend/src/app.ts; orangeadmin/app/src/main/java/com/sixteenhalves/orangeadmin; orangemain/shared/src/commonMain/kotlin/org/whoismacy/mainconsumapp"),
(0, "Why is the POS offline-first?",
 "Kenyan retail tills often sit on unreliable mobile networks. The POS writes sales, stock movements and catalog changes to a local SQLDelight database first, then pushes them to the server whenever connectivity returns. The server resolves conflicts with last-write-wins, so a till never blocks a queue of customers waiting for a network round trip.",
 "orangemain/shared/src/commonMain/kotlin/org/whoismacy/mainconsumapp/data/sync/SyncService.kt; backend/src/routes/sync.ts"),
(0, "Describe the sync model in one sentence.",
 "Each device keeps a local replica, queues its own writes, and periodically pulls changes newer than the last server_version it saw while pushing its pending records; the server applies pushes under last-write-wins on updated_at and stamps every accepted write with a new server_version for the next pull.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts; orangemain/shared/src/commonMain/kotlin/org/whoismacy/mainconsumapp/data/remote/BackendApi.kt"),
(0, "How is multi-branch scoping enforced?",
 "Every branch-owned table carries branch_id, and every authenticated request resolves the caller's allowed branches from the JWT claims. OWNER bypasses the branch filter; MANAGER requests are checked against branch_ids, and mismatches are rejected with BRANCH_SCOPE_MISMATCH. Reports re-verify scope with a dedicated helper before running any aggregate.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (scope helpers); backend/src/routes/stats.ts — scopeBranch(); backend/src/lib/jwt.ts"),
(0, "How are payments handled today?",
 "A sale is paid either by cash (marked PAID at the till) or by M-Pesa STK push. For M-Pesa the server initiates the prompt, keeps the sale PENDING, and marks it PAID only when the Daraja callback confirms success. Anything anomalous — late confirmation, missing receipt, unknown checkout ID — goes into the reconciliation queue for a human to resolve.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts; backend/src/payments/daraja.ts; backend/src/routes/reconciliation.ts"),
(0, "Why this backend stack?",
 "TypeScript + Fastify + PostgreSQL. TypeScript gives one language across the API and tooling; Fastify is lightweight and fast with good plugin lifecycle; PostgreSQL provides the transactional guarantees a money ledger needs (savepoints, unique constraints, sequences). Everything runs on Render's free tier, so the footprint was kept deliberately small — no Redis, no message queue.",
 "backend/package.json; backend/src/app.ts; backend/src/lib/db.ts"),
(0, "What is the demo scope for this presentation?",
 "Steps 1–3 are implemented and verified: the v1 backend with auth, catalog, devices, sync, payments, reconciliation and reports; the admin Android app; and the KMP POS with offline sync, credential setup and checkout. Known blockers are documented honestly — chiefly the Daraja sandbox app missing the STK product, which is a configuration fix before a live demo.",
 "project-description-revised.md; CHANGELOG.md §1 and §8; backend/src/routes"),
(0, "How are multiple tills per branch supported?",
 "Each till is a device row tied to a branch, provisioned with a one-time-issued token and a human-readable device code. Devices can be revoked instantly, hard-deleted after revocation, and report a heartbeat with their pending-outbox depth and app version so the admin can spot tills that have stopped syncing.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (device); backend/migrations/002_reconciliation_and_heartbeat.sql; backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (§ devices)"),

(1, "What is the overall architecture pattern?",
 "A modular monolith: one Fastify process exposing a versioned REST API, two mobile clients, and PostgreSQL as the store. Inside the backend, routes are thin HTTP layers; shared concerns live in src/lib (db, jwt, errors, audit, rate limit, ids, time); external integrations live in src/payments (Daraja). This keeps a small team productive without microservice overhead.",
 "backend/src/app.ts; backend/src/lib; backend/src/payments/daraja.ts"),
(1, "Why Fastify instead of Express?",
 "Fastify 5 gives a predictable plugin model, first-class async handlers, structured logging via pino, and very low per-request overhead — all useful on a free-tier instance. Request bodies are validated explicitly with zod wherever money or identity is involved, so we get strict parsing without coupling route code to a schema framework.",
 "backend/package.json (fastify ^5); backend/src/app.ts; backend/src/routes/payments.ts (zod schemas)"),
(1, "How is dependency injection handled in the backend?",
 "A Deps interface carries the database queryable, config, clock, token service and rate limiter, and a Guards interface carries auth helpers. Routes are registered with these dependencies, so tests can inject a fake clock, a test database, or wrapped queries without touching global state.",
 "backend/src/app.ts (Deps, Guards); backend/test/helpers.ts"),
(1, "How is configuration managed?",
 "config.ts parses environment variables at startup — database URL, JWT secret and issuer, access/refresh TTLs, Daraja credentials, callback secret, STK timeout, seed-owner credentials. Missing critical values fail fast at boot instead of surfacing as confusing 500s later. Render supplies the values as environment variables because the free tier has no shell.",
 "backend/src/config.ts; backend/src/index.ts"),
(1, "How does the admin app communicate with the API?",
 "Retrofit2 over OkHttp with Gson converters. NetworkModule builds the client with the bearer interceptor, an authenticator for 401 refresh, and the base URL https://y4p.onrender.com/. NetworkService declares every v1 endpoint as a suspend function returning typed DTO envelopes.",
 "orangeadmin/app/src/main/java/com/sixteenhalves/orangeadmin/di/NetworkModule.kt; .../ntwk/NetworkService.kt"),
(1, "How does client-side token refresh work?",
 "AuthInterceptor attaches the access token to every request. When a call returns 401, AuthAuthenticator synchronously calls POST /auth/refresh with the stored refresh token, saves the new pair, and retries the original request once. If refresh fails, the session is cleared and the user is sent back to login.",
 "orangeadmin/app/src/main/java/com/sixteenhalves/orangeadmin/ntwk/AuthInterceptor.kt; .../ntwk/AuthAuthenticator.kt; .../domain/SessionManager.kt"),
(1, "What is the POS technology stack?",
 "Kotlin Multiplatform with Compose Multiplatform for shared UI, Koin for dependency injection, SQLDelight for the local database, and Ktor for HTTP. The androidApp module is a thin shell hosting the shared Compose app; platform-specific pieces (database driver, clipboard) are provided through expect/actual declarations.",
 "orangemain/shared/build.gradle.kts; orangemain/shared/src/commonMain/kotlin/org/whoismacy/mainconsumapp; orangemain/androidApp"),
(1, "Why choose Kotlin Multiplatform for the POS?",
 "The POS holds the most business-critical logic — sale assembly, sync, credential parsing — and we want it written and tested once. KMP lets that logic live in commonMain with Android supplying only platform glue, leaving the door open to iOS later without a rewrite. The admin app stays pure Android because it has no such cross-platform ambition.",
 "orangemain/shared/src/commonMain/kotlin/org/whoismacy/mainconsumapp/App.kt; .../Platform.kt; orangemain/shared/src/androidMain"),
(1, "How is local persistence done on the POS?",
 "SQLDelight defines the schema; DatabaseDriverFactory is an expect declaration with an Android actual that opens the SQLite database in app storage. Repository classes (ProductRepository, CategoryRepository, TransactionRepository, CartRepository, SettingsRepository) wrap generated queries so UI code never touches SQL directly.",
 "orangemain/shared/src/commonMain/kotlin/org/whoismacy/mainconsumapp/data/local/DatabaseDriverFactory.kt; orangemain/shared/src/androidMain/.../DatabaseDriverFactory.android.kt; .../data/repository"),
(1, "What is the admin app's internal architecture?",
 "MVVM with Compose: MainViewModel coordinates state and calls AdminRepository, which talks to NetworkService and persists read snapshots in AdminSnapshotStore. Screens are stateless composables observing state; navigation is centralised in nav/MainRoutes.kt and MainAppShell. Session, theme, biometric and payment preferences have dedicated repositories.",
 "orangeadmin/app/src/main/java/com/sixteenhalves/orangeadmin/viewmodels/MainViewModel.kt; .../repository/AdminRepository.kt; .../nav/MainRoutes.kt"),
(1, "Where is the single source of truth for data shapes?",
 "DATA_CONTRACT.md (locked) defines entity shapes and mutation rules; API_CONTRACT.md defines transport, endpoints, error codes and sync/payment flows; the SQL migrations are the executable truth. Client DTOs mirror the snake_case JSON exactly, and backend zod schemas mirror the contract's validation rules.",
 "DATA_CONTRACT.md; API_CONTRACT.md; backend/migrations/001_init.sql; backend/src/routes"),
(1, "How is API versioning handled?",
 "The version lives in the path — /api/v1 — and within v1 only additive changes are allowed: new optional fields, new endpoints, new error codes. Breaking changes require /api/v2 alongside v1. Error codes are stable identifiers while human-readable messages may change freely.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §0; backend/src/app.ts"),
(1, "How is money represented?",
 "Money is stored and transported as integer cents everywhere — price_cents, total_amount_cents, amount_received_cents — never as floating point. This avoids rounding drift in totals, change calculations and M-Pesa amount comparisons, which the server checks against the sale total before initiating a prompt.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (product, sale); backend/src/routes/payments.ts (AMOUNT_MISMATCH)"),
(1, "How are timestamps handled?",
 "All timestamps are RFC 3339 UTC; client-supplied timestamps are stored as-is for LWW comparison, but ordering, token expiry, rate limits and STK timeouts always use server time. Device clocks that run far ahead are rejected outright (clock-skew guard) because otherwise a single wrong clock could win every conflict.",
 "API_CONTRACT.md §0; backend/src/lib/time.ts; backend/src/routes/sync.ts (clockSkewError)"),

(2, "What are the main database tables?",
 "app_user, app_user_branch, refresh_token, idempotency_key, branch, category, product, device, sale, sale_item, payment_transaction, daraja_callback, reconciliation_queue, plus an audit log and the server_version_seq sequence. Migration 002 adds a uniqueness rule for the reconciliation queue and device heartbeat columns.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql; backend/migrations/002_reconciliation_and_heartbeat.sql"),
(2, "How are users stored and what roles exist?",
 "app_user holds id, name, email (unique), password_hash (Argon2id), role (OWNER or MANAGER), and soft-delete/timestamps. Managers get their branch permissions through app_user_branch. Cashiers are not rows in app_user; they exist per sale.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (app_user, app_user_branch); backend/src/lib/jwt.ts"),
(2, "Why is there a separate app_user_branch table?",
 "A manager can be responsible for several branches, and a branch can have several managers — a many-to-many relation. Storing it in a join table keeps role checks cheap (the login handler loads branch_ids once) and avoids duplicating arrays in the user row that would be hard to query and constrain.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (app_user_branch); backend/src/routes/auth.ts (login claims)"),
(2, "How are refresh tokens modelled?",
 "Each row stores a cryptographic hash of the token, a family_id, expiry, rotated_at and revoked_at, plus user agent and IP for forensics. Rotation creates a new row in the same family and marks the old one rotated; family-level revocation lets the server respond to suspected theft by killing every descendant token at once.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (refresh_token, indexes); backend/src/routes/auth.ts"),
(2, "What does the idempotency_key table do?",
 "It stores a client-supplied Idempotency-Key together with the stored response for mutating requests. If the client retries — common on flaky mobile networks — the server replays the original response instead of executing the write twice. This complements the inherent idempotency of sync pushes, which are keyed by record id.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (idempotency_key); backend/src/lib/idempotency.ts; API_CONTRACT.md §0"),
(2, "What does the branch table contain?",
 "id, name, code, optional location, is_active, created_at, updated_at and sync bookkeeping. Branch deletion is refused while an active (non-revoked) device is attached, preventing a till from being orphaned from its catalog.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (branch); backend/src/routes/catalog.ts"),
(2, "How are categories modelled and constrained?",
 "Each category belongs to exactly one branch, carries a name and server_version, and is unique per branch among non-deleted rows. This prevents two cashiers from creating the same 'Drinks' category on different tills; the database constraint, not the UI, is the final arbiter.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (category, category_branch_version_idx); backend/src/routes/catalog.ts"),
(2, "What does the product table hold?",
 "id, branch_id, optional category_id, name, optional sku, price_cents, stock_quantity, is_active, deleted_at, server_version and timestamps. SKUs are unique per branch. Stock decrements happen inside the sale-apply savepoint so the ledger and inventory cannot diverge on a partial failure.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (product, indexes); backend/src/routes/sync.ts (applySale)"),
(2, "What is server_version and why does it exist?",
 "It is a per-row value assigned from a global PostgreSQL sequence (server_version_seq) on every accepted write. Devices pull rows with server_version greater than the last value they saw, which gives a simple, gap-tolerant incremental cursor without relying on wall-clock timestamps that devices can get wrong.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (server_version_seq, *_branch_version_idx); backend/src/routes/sync.ts"),
(2, "Which columns make up a sale?",
 "id, receipt_number, branch_id, device_id, cashier_id and cashier_name, payment_method and payment_status, subtotal/tax/total cents, amount_received and change cents, optional mpesa_receipt, client created_at, server received_at and server_version. Indexes cover branch+created_at (reporting) and PENDING status (timeout sweeps).",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (sale, sale_branch_created_idx, sale_payment_status_idx)"),
(2, "Why snapshot product names and prices into sale_item?",
 "A receipt must remain truthful after the catalog changes. sale_item stores product_id plus the name, unit price and line total as sold, so later price edits or product deletion never rewrite history. The product_id link stays for analytics and stock accounting.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (sale_item); backend/src/routes/sync.ts (applySale)"),
(2, "What does payment_transaction track?",
 "One row per STK attempt: the sale it belongs to, phone number, amount, merchant/checkout request ids from Daraja, status (PENDING/SUCCESS/FAILED), any M-Pesa receipt, and timestamps. A partial index on PENDING rows makes the timeout sweeper cheap.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (payment_transaction, pt_pending_idx)"),
(2, "Why store every Daraja callback raw?",
 "daraja_callback keeps the verbatim JSON body alongside parsed checkout ids. Daraja redelivers and semantics evolve; keeping the raw payload means disputes and reconciliation can be investigated from evidence, not from what our parser happened to understand at the time.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (daraja_callback); backend/src/routes/payments.ts (callback handler)"),
(2, "What is the reconciliation queue for?",
 "It captures money anomalies that need a human decision: a sale confirmed late, a success without a receipt, an unknown checkout id, an amount mismatch. One row per payment_transaction is enforced by a unique index, so repeated callback deliveries cannot flood the queue with duplicates.",
 "backend/migrations/002_reconciliation_and_heartbeat.sql; backend/src/routes/reconciliation.ts"),
(2, "How is a device represented?",
 "device holds branch_id, name, a human-readable device_code, a hash of the issued token, the token expiry, is_revoked, last_seen_at, app_version and timestamps. Provisioning returns the plaintext token once; from then on only its SHA-256 hash exists server-side.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (device); backend/migrations/002_...sql (app_version); backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (devices)"),
(2, "Why are there two deletion styles?",
 "Products and categories use soft delete (deleted_at) so the tombstone can replicate to offline tills, which must learn that an item disappeared. Devices are different: revocation is the soft step that cuts access, and a separate permanent-delete endpoint removes the row once the device is being retired.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql; backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (product delete, device revoke/delete); backend/src/routes/sync.ts (tombstones)"),
(2, "Why use application-generated UUIDs instead of serial IDs?",
 "Offline tills must create records (sales, categories, products) without asking the server. Client-generated IDs remove that round trip and make sync pushes naturally idempotent: the same row pushed twice collides on its primary key instead of creating a duplicate. IDs are minted centrally in lib/ids.ts.",
 "backend/src/lib/ids.ts; orangemain/shared/src/commonMain/kotlin/org/whoismacy/mainconsumapp/data/model"),
(2, "Which foreign keys and constraints protect the ledger?",
 "sale_item references sale (deleted with it) and product; sale references branch and device; payment_transaction references sale. Unique constraints cover receipt numbers, per-branch SKUs, per-branch category names, idempotency keys and one reconciliation row per payment attempt.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql; backend/migrations/002_reconciliation_and_heartbeat.sql"),
(2, "How is stock kept consistent with sales?",
 "When a pushed sale is applied, its line items decrement product stock inside the same SAVEPOINT as the sale insert. Either the sale and all its stock movements commit, or none do. The API reports per-line warnings so a till learns about missing products or price drift without breaking the whole batch.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts (inSavepoint, applySale)"),
(2, "How are deletions propagated to tills?",
 "Deleted products and categories retain a deleted_at tombstone and receive a new server_version. The next pull returns them just like any other changed row, and the device removes them locally. LWW applies to tombstones too, so a stale edit cannot resurrect a deleted item.",
 "backend/src/routes/sync.ts (tombstone handling, recordStaleWrite)"),
(2, "What is stored with an idempotency key?",
 "The key, the owning scope, when it was first seen, and the response body that was returned, so a replay returns byte-identical results. Records expire or are pruned by scope so the table stays bounded on a free-tier database.",
 "backend/src/lib/idempotency.ts; backend/migrations/001_init.sql (idempotency_key)"),
(2, "Which indexes exist for reporting?",
 "sale_branch_created_idx (branch_id, created_at DESC) powers day-range reports and sales lists; the partial sale_payment_status_idx on PENDING supports timeout sweeps; product_branch_version_idx and category_branch_version_idx support incremental pulls; sale_item_sale_idx supports receipt assembly.",
 "backend/migrations/001_init.sql (indexes); backend/src/routes/stats.ts"),
(2, "How does the database express low stock?",
 "It does not store a flag; low stock is a query condition — stock_quantity <= LOW_STOCK_THRESHOLD, where the constant is 5. That keeps the definition in one place, lets the threshold change without a migration, and makes the reports and dashboard agree by construction.",
 "backend/src/routes/stats.ts (LOW_STOCK_THRESHOLD, branchToday)"),
(2, "What is the migration strategy?",
 "Plain numbered SQL files applied in order by scripts/migrate.ts (also runnable manually with psql). There is no ORM and no auto-generated schema; migrations are reviewed like code. Migration 002 shows the additive pattern — CREATE UNIQUE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS and ADD COLUMN IF NOT EXISTS — so re-running is safe.",
 "backend/migrations/*.sql; backend/src/scripts/migrate.ts"),

(3, "Walk me through the login flow.",
 "POST /auth/login validates the zod body, looks up the user by email, verifies the Argon2id hash, and returns a 15-minute access JWT plus a 30-day refresh token, along with the user's id, name, email, role and branch_ids. Unknown emails still perform a dummy Argon2 verify so response timing does not reveal which accounts exist.",
 "backend/src/routes/auth.ts; backend/src/lib/jwt.ts"),
(3, "Why Argon2id for passwords?",
 "Argon2id is the current recommended password hash: memory-hard against GPU cracking and hybrid-resistant. It is used through the native @node-rs/argon2 binding, and verification is constant-time-ish via the library. Password material never leaves the server and hashes never leave the database.",
 "backend/package.json (@node-rs/argon2); backend/src/routes/auth.ts"),
(3, "What do the access token claims contain?",
 "sub (user id), role (OWNER or MANAGER), branch_ids, typ=access, issuer and a 15-minute expiry. HS256 signed with a shared secret. Routes derive scoping exclusively from these claims, so a tampered role or branch list cannot grant access.",
 "backend/src/lib/jwt.ts; backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (guards)"),
(3, "Why HS256 rather than RS256?",
 "The API is a single backend service that both signs and verifies; there is no third party validating tokens. A shared secret keeps deployment simple on the free tier. RSA would add key management with no benefit until another service needs offline verification.",
 "backend/src/lib/jwt.ts; backend/src/config.ts"),
(3, "Why are access tokens short-lived?",
 "Fifteen minutes bounds the damage of a leaked access token; revocation of a JWT is not instant, so the window must be small. Refresh tokens carry the long-lived session and are checked against the database on every refresh, which is where revocation actually happens.",
 "backend/src/lib/jwt.ts; backend/src/routes/auth.ts (refresh); API_CONTRACT.md §1.1"),
(3, "How does refresh-token rotation work?",
 "POST /auth/refresh hashes the presented token, finds its row, and if valid inserts a new token in the same family while marking the old row rotated. The client persists the new pair. A token can therefore be used exactly once, and any reuse is detectable.",
 "backend/src/routes/auth.ts (refresh handler); backend/migrations/001_init.sql (refresh_token)"),
(3, "What happens if a refresh token is replayed?",
 "The server treats reuse as theft: it revokes every token in that family, writes an audit event auth.refresh_reuse_detected with the family and user, and returns 401 REFRESH_TOKEN_REUSED. Everyone legitimately holding that session is forced to log in again.",
 "backend/src/routes/auth.ts (refresh reuse); backend/src/lib/audit.ts"),
(3, "How does logout work?",
 "POST /auth/logout requires the bearer token and the refresh token, then revokes the refresh token's family. The 15-minute access token becomes unusable at the client immediately because it is discarded, and worst case expires shortly after. Response is 204.",
 "backend/src/routes/auth.ts (logout); API_CONTRACT.md §1.1"),
(3, "What is /auth/me for?",
 "It restores the session on app launch: given a valid access token it returns id, name, email, role and branch_ids so the admin app can rebuild navigation and scoping without caching stale role data locally. The admin SessionManager uses it after loading stored tokens.",
 "backend/src/routes/auth.ts; API_CONTRACT.md §1.1; orangeadmin/.../domain/SessionManager.kt"),
(3, "How are new admin accounts created?",
 "There is no public registration. An OWNER calls the user-creation endpoint with name, email, password, role and branch_ids; MANAGER accounts must be given at least one branch. Self-service sign-up is intentionally absent because a retail back office is a closed group.",
 "backend/src/routes/auth.ts (register/invite handler); API_CONTRACT.md §1.1"),
(3, "How is a MANAGER restricted to their branches?",
 "Login and /auth/me resolve branch_ids from app_user_branch into the JWT. Every endpoint touching a branch checks those claims; a request for another branch gets BRANCH_SCOPE_MISMATCH. Reports additionally use a scopeBranch helper so aggregates can't be teased out by tweaking query parameters.",
 "backend/src/routes/stats.ts (scopeBranch); backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (scope checks); backend/src/lib/jwt.ts"),
(3, "What does account deletion do?",
 "DELETE /auth/account re-confirms the password, then soft-deletes the user: the row stays for audit integrity (sales reference cashier ids) while login and tokens stop working. Sessions are revoked. Hard deletion is deliberately not offered for humans in the ledger domain.",
 "backend/src/routes/auth.ts; API_CONTRACT.md §1.1"),
(3, "How are device tokens protected?",
 "Provisioning mints an opaque dvc_ token and returns it once; the server stores only its SHA-256 hash plus an expiry. Device-scoped calls hash the presented token and compare. If a device is revoked, authentication fails even if the holder still has the string.",
 "backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (devices, sha256Hex); backend/migrations/001_init.sql (device.token_hash)"),
(3, "How is the Daraja callback authenticated?",
 "Every callback must carry the shared secret either in the X-Callback-Secret header or the ?secret= query parameter; otherwise 401 CALLBACK_UNAUTHORIZED. The secret is embedded in the registered callback URL and configured via DARAJA_CALLBACK_URL. Unauthenticated callbacks are rejected before touching payment state.",
 "backend/src/routes/payments.ts (callback); backend/src/config.ts"),
(3, "Is there rate limiting?",
 "Yes — a small in-process fixed-window limiter protects sensitive endpoints. It is deliberately simple and single-node; the code documents that it must move to Redis when more than one instance serves traffic. This is honest v1 engineering, not a production-grade WAF.",
 "backend/src/lib/rate-limit.ts; backend/src/app.ts"),
(3, "How is request validation done?",
 "With zod schemas parsed at the route boundary via a parse() helper that converts failures into the standard error envelope with ISSUES details. Money-critical inputs — phone numbers, amounts, payment statuses — are validated before any side effect, and unknown fields are not merrily trusted.",
 "backend/src/app.ts (parse); backend/src/routes/payments.ts; backend/src/routes/sync.ts (schemas)"),
(3, "What does the error envelope look like and why?",
 "{\"error\": {\"code\", \"message\", \"details\"}}. Codes like BRANCH_SCOPE_MISMATCH or STK_IN_PROGRESS are stable machine identifiers the clients branch on; messages are human text that may change. details carries field-level context without breaking the shape.",
 "backend/src/lib/errors.ts; API_CONTRACT.md §0"),
(3, "Why no self-service sign-up?",
 "The threat model is theft of stock and money, not subscriber growth. A closed invite model lets an OWNER control who can see branch financials, simplifies abuse handling, and avoids building email-verification infrastructure for a system whose users are known employees.",
 "backend/src/routes/auth.ts (invite-only creation); API_CONTRACT.md §1.1"),
(3, "Where do secrets live?",
 "They are environment variables parsed by config.ts — JWT secret, database URL, Daraja consumer key/secret/passkey/shortcode, callback secret, seed-owner credentials. On Render they are set in the dashboard because the free tier has no shell; locally they come from the developer environment. None are committed to the repository.",
 "backend/src/config.ts; README/deployment notes"),
(3, "How does the admin app store tokens?",
 "In EncryptedSharedPreferences: AES-256-SIV for keys, AES-256-GCM for values, backed by the Android Keystore. If decryption fails (for example after a backup restore on another device), SessionManager resets the secure store and forces login instead of crashing.",
 "orangeadmin/app/src/main/java/com/sixteenhalves/orangeadmin/domain/SessionManager.kt"),
(3, "Why hash tokens instead of encrypting them?",
 "Hashes are one-way: even a database leak does not hand an attacker usable sessions. Verification only needs equality, so SHA-256 of the presented string is sufficient. The same approach is used for refresh tokens and device tokens, keeping the pattern uniform.",
 "backend/src/routes/auth.ts (token_hash); backend/src/routes/catalog.ts (sha256Hex)"),
(3, "How does the client react to a 401 mid-flow?",
 "OkHttp's Authenticator intercepts it, calls /auth/refresh once, and retries with the new access token; concurrent calls are coalesced so a single refresh serves them all. If refresh itself fails, the authenticator aborts, SessionManager clears the session, and navigation returns to the login screen.",
 "orangeadmin/.../ntwk/AuthAuthenticator.kt; .../ntwk/AuthInterceptor.kt; .../domain/SessionManager.kt"),

(3, "What was fixed in the authentication work after the first demo?",
 "Session crypto recovery was restored so stored credentials survive process death and library changes, and the seeded account is now promoted to OWNER at startup instead of silently duplicating. Device revocation and owner seeding landed together so a fresh deployment could bootstrap an owner and immediately manage tills.",
 "backend/src/scripts/bootstrap-owner.ts; commit cbc364d; commit fa3e76e; commit 94e3ee2"),
(3, "How does the seeded owner bootstrap work?",
 "On startup the bootstrap script creates the owner account if it is missing, or promotes the existing seeded account to OWNER and refreshes its password hash from SEED_ADMIN_* env values. This makes a fresh database usable without shell access, which matters on Render's free tier.",
 "backend/src/scripts/bootstrap-owner.ts; backend/src/index.ts; backend/src/scripts/seed-owner.ts"),
]
